\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Elementary abelian directions are Clifford}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring and let
#{G=(\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z)^n}. Write elements as vectors with
coordinates in #{\{0,1\}}. For units #{q_1,\ldots,q_n} of #{k} set
##{F(x,y)=(-1)^{\sum_{j<i}x_i y_j}\prod_{i=1}^n q_i^{x_i y_i}.}
Then #{F} is a #{2}-cocycle, and #{k_F[G]} is isomorphic to the
Clifford algebra of the diagonal form #{q(e_i)=q_i}. The unit-weight
derived form on the calculus with those directions is the case
#{q_i=1}. This is the shape of
\citet{Proposition 2.1}{albuquerque2002clifford} and
\citet{Proposition 2.2}{albuquerque2002clifford}, proved here over
any commutative ring.}

\proof{
\p{The exponent of #{(-1)} is an integer, so the value #{(-1)} is
the ring element, including in characteristic #{2} where it equals
#{1}. For the cocycle identity compare exponents of each #{q_i} and
of #{-1}, using representatives #{0} and #{1}. The group sum is
the exclusive or #{x_i+y_i-2x_i y_i}. Both sides of the identity
then have #{q_i}-exponent
#{x_i y_i+x_i z_i+y_i z_i-2x_i y_i z_i}. The sign exponent differs
by an even integer, so the powers of #{-1} agree. Expanding the
product then gives
#{(u_x u_y)u_z=F(x,y)F(xy,z)\,u_{xyz}} and
#{u_x(u_y u_z)=F(y,z)F(x,yz)\,u_{xyz}}. The cocycle identity says
the coefficients agree, so the product is associative.}

\p{Write #{u_i} for the basis element of the standard generator
#{e_i}. Then #{u_i^2=q_i}. If #{i<j}, the sign exponent of
#{F(e_i,e_j)} vanishes and that of #{F(e_j,e_i)} equals #{1}, while
every #{q}-exponent vanishes. Hence
#{u_i u_j=-u_j u_i}. The assignment #{e_i\mapsto u_i} therefore
satisfies the square and anticommutator relations of
\ref{fdip-000P}. The universal property supplies a homomorphism from
the Clifford algebra into this associative algebra. An ordered
monomial on a subset #{S} maps to a unit times #{u_S}: reordering
contributes a sign, and that monomial contains no square. Distinct
subsets give distinct group elements, so the images of the #{2^n}
ordered monomials are a basis. The homomorphism is an isomorphism.}
}
